WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:07.676
Static typing checks every value's type before a program runs. Dynamic typing checks as each line executes.

00:07.876 --> 00:14.608
Osprey's Python flavor keeps def, lambda and f-strings, infers every type, and checks it at compile time.

00:15.358 --> 00:23.786
This function has no annotations. The compiler works out that host is a string, because endsWith takes one, and that label returns a string.

00:23.986 --> 00:25.704
Main calls it with two strings.

00:26.454 --> 00:29.287
It runs, and prints Kitchen, kitchen.local.

00:30.037 --> 00:31.760
Pass the port number by mistake.

00:31.960 --> 00:40.570
The build stops, with the line and the two types that disagree. In CPython, that mistake surfaces only when the line runs, if it ever does.

00:41.320 --> 00:46.076
To test this on a real program, I ported a Chromecast sender to Python flavor.

00:46.276 --> 00:53.134
Where the original raised a KeyError for a missing TV, the port returns a result, and every caller must handle it.

00:53.884 --> 00:58.485
That's twelve hundred lines, and more than half its functions carry no annotation at all.

00:59.235 --> 01:06.185
The original's functions run under CPython and write the expected results. The port must match every one of them.

01:06.385 --> 01:08.951
Two hundred and fifty two checks, none failed.

01:09.701 --> 01:20.484
Python flavor is Osprey, not CPython. An arbitrary Python program does not run unchanged. To call real Python libraries, use Osprey's Python interop.

01:21.234 --> 01:27.960
Install Osprey, run the sample, then pass it a number. Osprey is actively evolving. Try it on your projects.
